pr_comments:
@pganssle 
I have to admit I'm a bit puzzled about your comment on pytz not supporting fold. There is currently no pytz-specific code in the fold implementation in this PR, and everything works the same for pytz and dateutil: value, fold, and offset all match.
Am I missing a test case?

@pganssle 
How about this. If we raise on anything unambiguous being passed and remove fold from all the functions past `convert_datetime_to_tso`, would that alleviate the worst of your concerns?

I'd prefer to keep the current overriding behavior, but I will drop it if it can get us past this argument and toward merging.

@AlexKirko I think the best thing to do here is to treat `fold` like any other component of the `datetime`, which is to say that (at least for now), it is completely ignored in any situation other than constructing a `Timestamp` from components.

I get that the semantics are funky, but they're *already* funky, but I think that considering that right now passing *any* value for `fold` is currently ignored completely, maybe it's best to go for consistency and address the conflict issues for datetime components all together. I have filed #31930 to track the possibility of cleaning up the semantics of `pd.Timestamp`.

Doing it this way will be most consistent with the way `Timestamp` already works and if I understand correctly it should remove the need for `fold` as an argument to either of the functions in contention.

@pganssle 
Do you mean just error raising behavior or in general? So if we pass fold with unambiguous input, then it gets ignored, but if we pass it with naive `datetime` or `str` that doesn't include offset, then we try to set to supplied fold?

Or do you suggest we completely ignore `fold` unless we are doing something like `pd.Timestamp(year=2019, month=10, day=27, hour=1, minute=30, fold=1, tz=gettz('Europe/London'))`? So that, in the paradigm you outlie in #31930, fold is only valid for one of the overloaded constructors?

@pganssle 
Unfortunately, because `func_create` ties all the function signatures together, I had to change all of them at once.
I would also prefer to leave the performance issue alone for now and make another issue and PR to experiment and see how we can jolt Cython into proper behavior.

@jreback @pganssle @mroeschke 
Great! Thanks to everyone for all the effort you made to make the changes as robust as possible.  We didn't hit exactly 256 comments or 128 commits, but other than that I'm very happy with the result.
A special thank you to @pganssle for the in-depth research and discussion!
